<h2>The case and the reference</h2>
<p>The claim arose from the death of a passenger travelling in a private vehicle insured under an Act-only policy. The Tribunal at Latur awarded compensation in 2017 and directed the insurer to pay and recover from the owner; the Bombay High Court (Aurangabad Bench) in 2019 maintained the quantum but exonerated the insurer, holding the deceased&rsquo;s risk uncovered — leaving the claimants to execute against the owner, which years of effort had not achieved. A two-Judge Bench had separately referred the &ldquo;third party&rdquo; question for authoritative determination; the present three-Judge Bench answered both that question and the connected one about pay-and-recover directions without statutory backing.</p>

<h2>Question one: the limits of compulsory coverage</h2>
<p>The Court held the inquiry is not simply &ldquo;who is a third party&rdquo; but what risks Section 147 compels to be insured. An Act-only policy mirrors the statutory minimum, and the statutory minimum does not reach gratuitous occupants of private vehicles or pillion riders. Claimants in such cases must look to the owner — or to whatever wider policy the owner actually bought. The practical message for vehicle owners is blunt: the bare policy leaves friends and family travelling with you uncovered.</p>

<h2>Question two: Article 142 as the safety valve</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Content</th></tr>
  <tr><td>I. Established liability</td><td>A judicially determined, enforceable obligation to compensate — here, the owner&rsquo;s liability under the unchallenged award</td></tr>
  <tr><td>II. Exceptional necessity</td><td>Compelling circumstances making ordinary execution an inadequate remedy — an elderly widow, no income, a decade of fruitless enforcement</td></tr>
  <tr><td>III. Necessity and proportionality</td><td>The direction confined to the outstanding award and interest, after adjusting payments received</td></tr>
  <tr><td>IV. Fairness and recovery safeguards</td><td>The insurer heard, and armed with recovery through execution before the Tribunal against the owner, without a fresh suit</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The Court was explicit that satisfying the four requirements creates no automatic entitlement: Article 142 relief remains exceptional and cannot substitute for the statutory allocation of insurance liability. The insurer&rsquo;s exoneration on law was affirmed even as the modification was made in equity.</p>

<h2>What changes in MACT practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For claimants&rsquo; counsel:</strong> plead coverage realistically. Where the policy is Act-only and the victim a gratuitous occupant, build the case against the owner from the start — and reserve the pay-and-recover prayer for genuinely exceptional hardship, documented, not asserted.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For insurers:</strong> the exoneration line holds, but a judicially determined award, prolonged non-realisation and a vulnerable claimant can still produce a pay-first direction — with the recovery mechanism now standardised through the Tribunal&rsquo;s execution jurisdiction.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For vehicle owners:</strong> the judgment is a renewed warning that an Act-only premium saves money by transferring passenger risk onto the owner personally — including execution against personal assets.</div>
</div>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Delhi angle:</strong> MACT benches in Delhi routinely face the gratuitous-occupant issue in two-wheeler pillion claims; this judgment supplies the controlling framework for both the coverage defence and any exceptional-relief prayer.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. It is not legal advice; coverage and relief in motor accident claims depend on the policy and record in each case.</div>
